From 7ae1a4465324906cf870a7ba55a4381d779c17d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 17:40:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [AMD] DSA decode graphs: one variant on every attention-DP
 rank, one verify graph

On HIP, DSA models capture a dense (k-only indexer) and a sparse decode graph per
batch size, and each rank picked its variant from its own longest sequence. Under DP
attention the ranks replay in lockstep and the collectives captured inside the graphs
pair up across ranks; some register their graph's input buffers, so a rank replaying
the other variant reads its peer's stale buffers (garbled output on GLM-5.2 with
in-graph aiter all-gather / reduce-scatter, MI355X PD decode TP4 + DP4 attention).

- Target-verify captures hold one DSA graph: the dense variant only skips the
  decode/idle indexer logits, so both verify graphs ran the same kernels (and select()
  synced seq_lens to the host on every verify replay).
- The MLP sync row gains an upper bound of the rank's longest decode sequence (host
  lengths only); the maximum over ranks rides ScheduleBatch / ForwardBatch as
  dp_decode_graph_max_seq_len and the DSA, sequence-length bucket and DSv4.1
  candidate variants select from it in decode/idle. No new collective, no device read.
  Without the sync (no DP attention) the per-rank rule is unchanged.

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
